# D-Bus activation for org.freedesktop.Notifications.
#
# This does not START the shell, and that is the whole design. Activation
# normally means "run Exec to provide this name", but the thing that provides
# it here is a session shell the compositor launches once -- so an Exec that
# ran asteroidz-bar would put a SECOND bar and a second wallpaper on screen
# every time an application notified while the first one was still coming up.
#
# It waits instead, which is what KDE's plasma_waitforname does for exactly
# this reason. D-Bus holds the caller's Notify until the name is acquired by
# whoever acquires it, so a notification sent in the seconds between login and
# the bar claiming the name is delivered rather than answered with
# ServiceUnknown. Nothing is started that was not already starting.
#
# `gdbus wait` rather than a shim of our own: it is one glib2 binary already on
# every system this runs on, and a program whose entire job is to wait for a
# bus name is not worth writing twice.
#
# The timeout bounds the failure. Without one, an application notifying on a
# machine where the shell is genuinely not running would block forever instead
# of being told. Thirty seconds is longer than a login takes and shorter than
# anybody's patience.
[D-BUS Service]
Name=org.freedesktop.Notifications
Exec=/usr/bin/gdbus wait --session --timeout 30 org.freedesktop.Notifications
